<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Contestani</h1><p class="st-meta">500 BCE – 197 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Iberian people of Alicante and Murcia whose sculptors produced the Lady of Elche, the masterpiece of Iberian art.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Contestani occupied the coast and hinterland of south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, roughly the modern provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alicante">Alicante</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, from the 5th century BCE. They grew out of earlier Iberian communities in close contact with Phoenician and Greek traders.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their towns, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-alcudia-elche">Ilici</a> (La Alcudia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elche">Elche</a>), La Serreta near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcoy">Alcoy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucentum">Lucentum</a>, were centres of a refined Iberian culture. The stone bust of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lady-of-elche">Lady of Elche</a>, carved around the 4th century BCE, comes from their territory. They wrote their Iberian language in a Greek-derived alphabet, recorded on lead tablets, and traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, wine and esparto.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Carthaginian control under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/barcid-iberia">Barcid Iberia</a>, the region passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a> in 209 BCE, and was pacified by 197 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-alcudia-elche">Ilici</a> became a Roman colony, and the Contestani merged into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/contestani.jpg" alt="Contestani" width="320" height="298"><figcaption>Image: The Ogre · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ethnographic_Iberia_200_BCE.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 BCE – 197 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Iberian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Iberian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Contestanians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-348&amp;focus=contestani" aria-label="Contestani on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4578 734 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Contestani lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4975 1081h0M5031 967h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4898 990h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4898 990h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Spain. <a href="/atlas?year=-348&amp;focus=contestani">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
